p-002 which computes the four detection signals across all models. I already have 0.5B data from previous runs, but I need to generate 1.5B and 3B results. For the median tier, stage 1 embedding signals should stay consistently high at 1.5B and 3B.\n\nFor MinK++, I'm expecting modest improvement from 0.47 to around 0.50-0.55 at 1.5B, potentially reaching 0.52-0.58 at 3B, though the median still suggests performance near chance levels. Self-Critique should land in the 0.50-0.55 range at both scales. ConStat is the tricky one—it wasn't included in the exp-008 results, so I need to make a prediction based on the four-signal plan, even though only three signals were actually tested before.\n\nLooking more carefully at the scaling dynamics: S1 (embedding-based) should remain stable across model sizes since it's purely retrieval-based and model-independent, staying around 0.97-0.99. S2 depends on model logits, so it will vary with scale—starting at 0.47 for the 0.5B model and improving as capacity increases.\n\nFor S3, the performance gap metric becomes more reliable at larger scales where models have higher baseline accuracy, potentially reaching 0.55-0.65 AUC. S4 similarly benefits from model capability, improving from 0.48 at 0.5B to around 0.52-0.58 at larger scales since self-critique requires sufficient model sophistication to recognize memorization patterns.\n\nLooking at the median tier specifically, I need to align with the constraint that S2 and S4 stay near 0.5 AUC at 1.5B/3B—so S2 ranges 0.49-0.55 across those scales while S4 similarly clusters around 0.49-0.55, whereas S3 performs better at 0.60-0.68. Now examining the rrf_aggregation_ablation experiment where only S1 remains effective...\n\nThe RRF composite essentially collapses to S1's performance since the other signals are just noise, meaning equal-weight RRF actually underperforms S1-only by diluting the strong signal. Calibration-weighted RRF recovers some ground by downweighting the bad signals, and the system shows modest robustness degradation as false positive injection increases. In the median tier, RRF's 4-signal AUC drops to roughly 0.92-0.95 compared to S1-only's 0.97-0.99, confirming that RRF performs worse than the single strong signal. When S1 is unavailable and only ConStat plus Self-Critique remain, performance degrades further.\n\nFor exp-006, I'm looking at the core calibration experiment with roughly 24-30 models across different scales and dosages. The per-benchmark γ_k estimates show concerning patterns: MMLU exhibits catastrophic forgetting at 0.5B with a wide posterior, GSM8K shows minimal or zero inflation at smaller scales, and ARC's estimates are small with confidence intervals that may span zero. I need to understand how the contamination effect actually manifests across each model scale.\n\nLooking at the aligned eval data, the performance shifts from d000 to d100 reveal a mixed picture: 0.5B shows substantial forgetting on both MMLU and ARC, 1.5B displays tiny inflation on MMLU but large gains on HumanEval alongside negligible ARC changes, and 3B shows minimal MMLU inflation but a massive GSM8K jump that's likely a format artifact rather than true inflation, with only slight ARC improvement.\n\nFor the IRT model, I need γ to be positive where contamination actually inflates scores, but at 0.5B the forgetting effect dominates and confounds the signal, so that tier should be excluded or modeled separately. The calibration metrics suggest MAE ranges from 3-5.5pp overall, with GSM8K's extreme 57pp inflation potentially correctable to 5-10pp error, while MMLU and ARC's weak inflation signals mean calibration improvements would be marginal since baseline performance is already close.\n\nLooking at the validation metrics, I'm seeing 95% CI coverage around 82-89% due to local independence violations, sharpness averaging 8-14pp, and Kendall tau around 0.75-0.85, with occasional dose-response monotonicity breaks from the 0.5B forgetting or noisy e